  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nLatest water vapor imagery shows our fully mature and occluded\nnor'easter heading towards the NJ Coast early this morning with\nrapidly cooling cloud tops in its northwest quadrant as a deep\nmoisture fetch from the Caribbean continues to wrap around the\ncenter. As the storm inches closer to the coast over the coming\nhours, high res guidance is in good agreement indicating that\nrain bands will continue to rotate north and westward into more\nof eastern NY and western New England. The most widespread \nareas of rain look to occur this morning before rain bands lift \nfurther north and west out of our area as drier air aloft wraps \ninto the storm, allowing rain to transition to just \nintermittent, patchy showers. The incoming drier air aloft \ncombined with downsloping effects from breezy north-northeast \nwinds should even support periods of mainly dry conditions this \nafternoon. The only exception looks to be the northeast facing \nslopes of the eastern Catskills and eastern slopes of the \nsouthern Greens/Taconics/Berkshires as upslope flow supports \ncontinued steady/moderate rain. We will note that showalter \nvalues trend towards zero this afternoon within the dry slot as \n850-500 hPa lapse rates steepen to 5.5-5.8C/km. Therefore, \ncontinue to mention slight chance thunder this afternoon as it \nis not out of the question for some rumbles of thunder if an \nupdraft can grow tall enough. \n\nOtherwise, as our nor'easter nears the coast and gains \nproximity this morning, forecast soundings continue to show an \ninversion that should prevent the impressive ~50kt low-level \njet from fulling mixing to the sfc; however, there appears to \nbe sufficient boundary layer mixing to support gusts 25 to 35mph\nwith even brief instances up to 40-45mph in the wind advisory \narea (especially in NW CT and higher terrain areas of western \nMA). Latest probabilistic guidance shows the highest potential \nfor gusts to exceed 30mph remains in areas mainly south and east\nof the Capital District through 15 UTC before probabilities \nquickly trend much lower through the afternoon as the coastal \nlow fills in. There was consideration to cancel the wind \nadvisory as probabilities for wind gusts to exceed 40mph is low \n(under 30%) but we felt we should keep the advisory out as the \ncoastal low reaches it closest proximity before deciding to \ncancel.\n\nPatchy, intermittent showers tonight trend into steadier \nperiods of rain by early Monday morning as our coastal low \nbecomes vertically stacked and lifts into New England. The \ndreary weather continues into Monday as our coastal low weakens \nfurther with lingering moisture under the broadening parent \ntrough support continued rain showers through the day. Total \nrainfall through 8PM Monday ranges between 0.5 and 2 inches with\nthe highest amounts in Litchfield County, east facing slopes of\nthe Berkshires, Taconics, southern Greens, northern and eastern\nCatskills. In fact, latest probabilistic guidance shows a \ngreater than 75% chance for total rainfall amounts to exceed 2 \ninches in the northern/eastern Catskills with even a 30 to 60% \nchance for over 3 inches of rain due to upslope enhancements. \nWhile such rainfall amounts can lead to nuisance flooding in \nlow-lying and poor drainage areas, latest river forecasts and \nensembles from MMEFS output do not indicate any river flooding \nconcerns. Drier conditions return Tuesday into the middle of \nnext week with temperatures also trending slightly warmer than \nnormal.",
